  <h1>Dengue NS1 Rapid Test vs PCR</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> The NS1 rapid test gives a point-of-care result in minutes at lower cost; RT-PCR is the higher-sensitivity reference that runs in a laboratory. Rapid NS1 screens fast; PCR confirms.</p>

  <h2>Comparison table</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>NS1 antigen rapid test</th><th>RT-PCR</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Target</th><td>NS1 viral protein</td><td>Viral RNA (nucleic acid)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Time</th><td>Minutes</td><td>Hours (lab)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Sensitivity</th><td>Lower (serotype-dependent)</td><td>Higher (gold standard)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Instrument</th><td>None</td><td>Thermal cycler</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cost</th><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Use</th><td>POC screening, early triage</td><td>Confirmation, low-viral-load cases</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What each method detects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NS1 antigen</strong> is a viral protein — present during active replication.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR</strong> detects viral RNA directly — the most sensitive and specific target.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Testing window</h2>
  <p>NS1 is best in the first week of fever; PCR also works early and can detect low viral loads later, including during secondary infection.</p>

  <h2>Strengths</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NS1 rapid:</strong> instrument-free, minutes, field-deployable, low cost.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR:</strong> highest sensitivity/specificity; detects low viremia and confirms serotypes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NS1:</strong> lower sensitivity, serotype variation, misses late/low-viral-load cases.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR:</strong> requires laboratory, trained staff, cold-chain reagents and higher cost.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>False negative / positive considerations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NS1 false negative:</strong> testing too late or low viremia.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR false negative:</strong> improper sample handling; rare false positives from contamination.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Confirmatory testing</h2>
  <p>A negative NS1 during clinical suspicion should be confirmed by PCR; PCR is itself the reference method.</p>

  <h2>Procurement considerations</h2>
  <p>Field programs favor NS1 rapid tests for screening and reserve PCR for reference laboratories.</p>

